%S A402372 0,2,26,776,28296,1472256,95558400,7968844800,805027507200,
%T A402372 99073555660800,14372025318604800,2455788321423360000,
%U A402372 483993089437777920000,109741445669333237760000,28213573586851457925120000,8200215556876995573841920000,2665854381604939727634432000000
%N A402372 Total sum of Hamming distances between the binary matrices of doubly shaded cells using construction from A395245, over all ordered pairs of permutations of [n].
%C A402372 Using the same shading construction as in A395245, associate to each permutation sigma the binary matrix M_sigma whose 1-entries mark the doubly shaded cells.
%C A402372 Let d(sigma,pi) be the Hamming distance between M_sigma and M_pi,different matrices.
%C A402372 Then a(n) is the sum of d(sigma,pi) over all ordered pairs (sigma,pi) in S_n x S_n.
%C A402372 Equivalently, if q_{ij} denotes the number of permutations for which cell (i,j) is doubly shaded, one has Sum q_{ij} = A395245(n) and a(n) = 2*(n!*A395245(n) - Sum q_{ij}^2). This can be easily proved.
%C A402372 The frequencies admit the closed form: q_{ij} = 0 when i is even or j is odd, q_{ij} = (n-2)!*((n-j+1)*(n-i+1)-n+max(i,j)-1) otherwise.
%C A402372 The same combinatorial counting that underlies the recurrence and generating function of A395245.
%F A402372 a(n) = 2*(n!*A395245(n) - Sum_{i=1..n, j=1..n} q_{ij}^2), where q_{ij} = (n-2)!*((n-j+1)*(n-i+1)-n+max(i,j)-1) when i is odd and j is even, and q_{ij}=0 otherwise.
%e A402372 For n=2: The two permutations produce the matrices with a single 1 at position (1,2) and the zero matrix, respectively. The 4 pairwise Hamming distances sum to 2, so a(2)=2.
%e A402372 For n=3: The six sets of doubly shaded cells are empty, {(1,2)}, {(1,2)}, {(1,2),(3,2)}, {(1,2)}, {(3,2)}. The sum of all 36 pairwise Hamming distances equals 26, so a(3)=26.
%e A402372 For n=4: Direct evaluation of the 24 matrices yields a(4)=776.
%o A402372 (Python)
%o A402372 from math import factorial
%o A402372 def A395245(n):
%o A402372     if n < 2:
%o A402372         return 0
%o A402372     a = [0] * (n + 1)
%o A402372     for k in range(1, n + 1):
%o A402372         a[k] = (k - 2) * a[k - 1] + factorial(k - 1) * (k * k // 4)
%o A402372     return a[n]
%o A402372 def q(i, j, n):
%o A402372     if i % 2 == 0 or j % 2 == 1:
%o A402372         return 0
%o A402372     return factorial(n - 2) * ((n - j + 1) * (n - i + 1) - n + max(i, j) - 1)
%o A402372 def a(n):
%o A402372     s = sum(q(i, j, n)**2 for i in range(1, n + 1) for j in range(1, n + 1))
%o A402372     return 2 * (factorial(n) * A395245(n) - s)
%Y A402372 Cf. A395245, A397145, A397137, A398003.
%K A402372 nonn,new
